    "longDesc": "Sedative, hypnotic or anxiolytic dependence with intoxication, uncomplicated",
    "plainEnglish": "This code describes a situation where someone has become physically or psychologically dependent on sedative, hypnotic, or anti-anxiety medications (like benzodiazepines or sleeping pills), and they are currently intoxicated or under the influence of these drugs without serious complications at this moment. Dependence means their body has adapted to the drug and they may experience withdrawal symptoms if they stop, while intoxication means they've taken enough of the substance to feel its effects acutely.",
    "whoUses": "Addiction medicine specialists, psychiatrists, primary care physicians, and substance abuse treatment providers use this code when documenting patients in treatment for medication dependence who present while actively intoxicated. This code typically appears on EOB statements when patients seek care at addiction treatment centers, psychiatric hospitals, or emergency departments for substance use disorder management.",
