    "longDesc": "Sedative, hypnotic or anxiolytic dependence with withdrawal, uncomplicated",
    "plainEnglish": "This diagnosis means you've developed a physical dependence on sedative, hypnotic, or anti-anxiety medications (such as benzodiazepines, barbiturates, or sleep aids) and are experiencing withdrawal symptoms without serious complications. When your body becomes physically dependent on these medications after regular use, stopping or reducing them can cause uncomfortable symptoms like anxiety, tremors, sweating, or insomnia—this is what \"withdrawal\" means. The \"uncomplicated\" part indicates you're having typical withdrawal effects rather than severe or life-threatening complications like seizures or dangerous changes in heart rate.",
    "whoUses": "Addiction medicine specialists, psychiatrists, primary care physicians, and substance abuse counselors use this code when treating patients going through withdrawal from sedative or anti-anxiety medications. It commonly appears on EOB statements for patients in detoxification programs, inpatient rehabilitation facilities, or outpatient addiction treatment, and may also be used during hospitalization when withdrawal needs medical monitoring.",
