    "plainEnglish": "This code indicates you have a history of substance use involving sedatives, hypnotics, or anti-anxiety medications (like benzodiazepines or sleeping pills), but you are currently in remission—meaning you are no longer actively using these substances and have achieved a period of recovery. This diagnosis is used when the specific pattern or severity of your past use isn't detailed in your medical record, but healthcare providers want to document your recovery status for ongoing treatment planning.",
    "whoUses": "Addiction medicine specialists, psychiatrists, primary care physicians, and mental health counselors use this code when treating patients with histories of sedative or anxiolytic misuse who are now in sustained recovery. This code occasionally appears on EOB statements when patients are receiving counseling, follow-up care, or management related to their substance use recovery, though it's more commonly used in clinical documentation than on patient-facing billing statements.",
