    "shortDesc": "Sedatv/hyp/anxiolytc use, unsp w withdrawal delirium",
    "longDesc": "Sedative, hypnotic or anxiolytic use, unspecified with withdrawal delirium",
    "plainEnglish": "This code describes a serious condition where someone who has been regularly using sedatives, sleeping pills, or anti-anxiety medications stops taking them and experiences delirium—a state of confusion, hallucinations, and disorientation—as a withdrawal symptom. When the body becomes dependent on these drugs and suddenly stops receiving them, it can trigger dangerous mental and physical symptoms including severe confusion, seeing or hearing things that aren't there, and rapid changes in awareness and mood.",
    "whoUses": "Psychiatrists, addiction medicine specialists, emergency medicine physicians, and hospitalists use this code when treating patients experiencing sedative-hypnotic withdrawal with delirium in hospital or psychiatric settings. It is less common on routine patient EOBs and more likely to appear on claims for inpatient psychiatric or emergency department visits, as withdrawal delirium requires immediate medical intervention.",
