    "plainEnglish": "This diagnosis describes someone who is actively abusing drugs or other psychoactive substances (not alcohol, cocaine, opioids, or other specifically named drugs) and is experiencing withdrawal symptoms that include hallucinations or other distorted perceptions. Withdrawal happens when someone stops or reduces use after regular abuse, and in this case, the person's brain is reacting so intensely that they're seeing, hearing, or sensing things that aren't real. This is a serious medical condition that requires prompt treatment to manage both the withdrawal and the disturbing hallucinations.",
    "whoUses": "Addiction medicine specialists, psychiatrists, emergency department physicians, and substance abuse treatment centers use this code when treating patients experiencing withdrawal with perceptual disturbances from polysubstance use or less common psychoactive substances. This code appears on patient EOBs when hospitalization or intensive outpatient treatment is needed for medically supervised detoxification with psychiatric complications.",
