    "rawCode": "F1921",
    "shortDesc": "Other psychoactive substance dependence, in remission",
    "longDesc": "Other psychoactive substance dependence, in remission",
    "plainEnglish": "This code means you have had a documented dependence or addiction to a psychoactive substance (such as inhalants, prescription drugs, or other drugs not specified as alcohol, opioids, cannabis, or stimulants), but you are currently in remission—meaning you are no longer actively using or meeting the criteria for active addiction. Remission can be early (within the first 3-12 months of stopping) or sustained (after 12 months or longer), and this code indicates you've made progress in your recovery.",
    "whoUses": "Addiction medicine specialists, psychiatrists, primary care physicians, and substance abuse counselors use this code when documenting ongoing treatment or recovery management. It commonly appears on insurance EOBs when patients are receiving medication-assisted treatment, counseling, or follow-up care for a history of substance dependence, as it helps justify continued treatment services even when active use has stopped.",
