    "longDesc": "Other psychoactive substance use, unspecified with withdrawal, uncomplicated",
    "plainEnglish": "This code describes someone who is withdrawing from an unspecified psychoactive substance (meaning a drug or medication that affects the brain and mood) in a straightforward way without serious complications. Withdrawal happens when someone stops using a substance they've been dependent on, and their body and brain react with uncomfortable symptoms like anxiety, sweating, insomnia, or irritability. The 'uncomplicated' part means the withdrawal isn't severe enough to cause dangerous medical emergencies or serious psychiatric crises.",
    "whoUses": "Addiction medicine specialists, psychiatrists, and emergency department physicians use this code when treating patients in withdrawal from recreational drugs or misused substances whose specific type isn't documented or identified. It commonly appears on EOBs for inpatient detoxification, emergency room visits, or outpatient addiction treatment, though the vague 'unspecified' nature means providers should ideally document the actual substance involved.",
