    "shortDesc": "Dysthymic disorder",
    "longDesc": "Dysthymic disorder",
    "plainEnglish": "Dysthymic disorder, now called persistent depressive disorder, is a long-lasting form of depression where you feel persistently sad, hopeless, or low in mood for at least two years (one year in children and teenagers). Unlike major depression which comes in episodes, dysthymia is more like a constant, lower-level depression that doesn't go away, though it may get slightly better or worse at different times. People with this condition often struggle with low energy, poor concentration, changes in appetite or sleep, and difficulty enjoying activities, but the symptoms aren't quite severe enough to be classified as major depression.",
    "whoUses": "Psychiatrists, psychologists, primary care physicians, and mental health counselors use this code when diagnosing and treating patients with chronic, long-term depression. It commonly appears on EOBs for therapy sessions, psychiatric evaluations, and mental health medication management visits, as it helps insurance companies understand the ongoing nature of the patient's mental health condition.",
