    "code": "F43.0",
    "rawCode": "F430",
    "shortDesc": "Acute stress reaction",
    "longDesc": "Acute stress reaction",
    "plainEnglish": "An acute stress reaction is your body's immediate emotional and physical response to a traumatic, frightening, or overwhelming event. Symptoms typically appear within minutes to hours after the triggering event and may include anxiety, panic, confusion, numbness, or difficulty concentrating—think of it as your nervous system's \"fight or flight\" alarm going into overdrive. These reactions are normal responses to abnormal situations and usually improve within a few days to weeks as you process what happened.",
    "whoUses": "Psychiatrists, psychologists, primary care physicians, and emergency medicine doctors commonly diagnose acute stress reactions in patients who have recently experienced trauma, accidents, loss, or other major stressors. This code frequently appears on EOB statements for emergency room visits, urgent care consultations, and mental health crisis evaluations following traumatic events.",
