    "shortDesc": "Post-traumatic stress disorder, acute",
    "longDesc": "Post-traumatic stress disorder, acute",
    "plainEnglish": "Post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) acute means you're experiencing severe anxiety, flashbacks, and emotional distress within the first few weeks after a traumatic event—such as an accident, assault, natural disaster, or combat exposure. Your brain and body are in a heightened state of alert, and you may have nightmares, intrusive memories, or avoid reminders of what happened. This is a normal response to extreme trauma, though the intensity of symptoms requires professional help to prevent the condition from becoming long-term.",
    "whoUses": "Psychiatrists, psychologists, trauma specialists, emergency medicine doctors, and primary care physicians use this code when treating patients in the acute phase following trauma. It commonly appears on EOB statements when mental health treatment, therapy sessions, or psychiatric evaluation are billed in the immediate aftermath of a traumatic event.",
