    "code": "F51.02",
    "rawCode": "F5102",
    "shortDesc": "Adjustment insomnia",
    "longDesc": "Adjustment insomnia",
    "plainEnglish": "Adjustment insomnia is sleep difficulty that develops as a direct response to a specific stressful life event or major change, such as a job loss, divorce, moving, or bereavement. Unlike chronic insomnia, this type of sleep problem is clearly tied to an identifiable trigger and typically improves once you've adapted to the new situation or the stressor resolves. You may experience trouble falling asleep, frequent nighttime wakings, or early morning awakening while your mind is processing the life change.",
    "whoUses": "Primary care physicians, psychiatrists, and sleep medicine specialists use this code when treating patients whose insomnia is directly caused by life stressors or adjustment periods. It commonly appears on patient EOBs when documenting sleep-related complaints during therapy or medical visits following a major life event.",
