    "rawCode": "F5103",
    "shortDesc": "Paradoxical insomnia",
    "longDesc": "Paradoxical insomnia",
    "plainEnglish": "Paradoxical insomnia is a sleep disorder where you believe you're not sleeping well or barely sleeping at all, even though objective measurements (like sleep studies) show you're actually getting a normal amount of sleep. Essentially, your perception of your sleep doesn't match the reality of how much sleep your body is actually getting. This can cause significant daytime worry and distress about your sleep quality, even though medically your sleep is adequate.",
    "whoUses": "Sleep medicine specialists, psychiatrists, and primary care physicians use this code when diagnosing patients who report severe insomnia symptoms but whose sleep study results contradict their complaints. It may appear on EOBs if a sleep study was performed, though the condition is somewhat controversial in modern medicine and is less commonly coded than other insomnia types.",
