    "plainEnglish": "Primary hypersomnia means you sleep excessively and feel persistently drowsy during the day, even after getting plenty of sleep at night—and this happens without an underlying medical condition, sleep disorder like sleep apnea, or medication causing it. People with this condition may sleep 10+ hours nightly and still struggle to stay awake during the day, which can affect work, school, and daily activities.",
    "whoUses": "Sleep medicine specialists and neurologists primarily diagnose and code primary hypersomnia, though psychiatrists and primary care physicians may also use it when evaluating chronic daytime sleepiness. This code commonly appears on patient EOBs when sleep studies are ordered or when treating excessive daytime somnolence that doesn't fit other sleep disorder categories.",
