    "shortDesc": "Hypersomnia due to other mental disorder",
    "longDesc": "Hypersomnia due to other mental disorder",
    "plainEnglish": "This diagnosis means you are sleeping excessively (much more than the typical 7-9 hours per night) specifically because of another mental health condition you have, such as depression, anxiety, or bipolar disorder. Rather than a sleep disorder on its own, your oversleeping is a symptom caused by the underlying mental health issue—your brain is using sleep as a response to emotional or psychological distress. You might find yourself sleeping 10+ hours a night, feeling unrefreshed when you wake, or struggling to stay awake during the day because your mental health condition is driving this sleep behavior.",
    "whoUses": "Psychiatrists, psychologists, primary care physicians, and sleep medicine specialists use this code when treating patients whose excessive daytime sleepiness or prolonged nighttime sleep is directly linked to a documented mental health disorder. It commonly appears on EOBs when mental health treatment is being billed, as it helps distinguish hypersomnia caused by psychiatric conditions from primary sleep disorders.",
