    "shortDesc": "Abuse of vitamins",
    "longDesc": "Abuse of vitamins",
    "plainEnglish": "This code describes the excessive or harmful use of vitamins beyond recommended doses, which can occur when someone compulsively takes high amounts of vitamin supplements despite potential health risks. While vitamins are generally considered safe and necessary for health, taking far more than your body needs—especially of fat-soluble vitamins like A, D, E, and K that accumulate in body tissues—can cause toxicity, organ damage, and other serious complications. This diagnosis is used when a healthcare provider identifies a pattern of vitamin abuse that's causing medical or behavioral problems.",
    "whoUses": "Psychiatrists, addiction medicine specialists, primary care physicians, and toxicologists use this code when evaluating patients with compulsive or problematic vitamin supplementation habits. It rarely appears on patient EOBs as a primary diagnosis but may be documented in cases of suspected substance abuse disorder or when vitamin toxicity requires clinical intervention.",
