    "code": "F80.0",
    "rawCode": "F800",
    "shortDesc": "Phonological disorder",
    "longDesc": "Phonological disorder",
    "plainEnglish": "A phonological disorder is a speech problem where a child has difficulty producing or organizing the sounds of speech correctly, even though they can physically make those sounds and understand language normally. For example, a child might consistently replace the 'r' sound with a 'w' sound (saying 'wabbit' instead of 'rabbit') or drop sounds from words in a pattern, making their speech harder to understand than expected for their age. This is different from having a hoarse voice or stuttering—it's specifically about how the brain organizes and uses individual speech sounds.",
    "whoUses": "Speech-language pathologists (SLPs) and pediatricians most commonly use this code when diagnosing and treating children with articulation or phonological delays. It appears in educational settings, developmental clinics, and speech therapy practices, and may appear on EOBs when speech therapy services are billed to insurance.",
