    "longDesc": "Rett's syndrome",
    "plainEnglish": "Rett syndrome is a rare genetic neurological disorder that almost exclusively affects girls, typically appearing when a child is 6 to 18 months old. After a period of normal development, children with Rett syndrome experience a regression where they lose purposeful hand skills, speech abilities, and social engagement, and often develop seizures, breathing problems, and movement difficulties. This devastating condition is caused by a mutation in the MECP2 gene on the X chromosome and results in severe intellectual disability and physical impairment throughout life.",
